    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm prepping a demo for my Year 10 chemistry class on the volatile compounds in Allium vegetables. Could you give me a detailed breakdown of the chemical pathway that occurs when an onion is sliced, specifically explaining how the enzymatic reaction leads to the production of propanethial S-oxide and why that particular molecule causes the stinging sensation in the eyes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you slice an onion, you physically damage the plant cells, causing the enzyme alliinase to react with the substrate alliin. This reaction produces the volatile compound propanethial S-oxide, which travels rapidly into the air when released from cut surfaces. The stinging sensation occurs because this molecule is both highly polar and capable of forming hydrogen bonds with the proteins in your cornea. Once in contact with your eyes, it causes water molecules to dissociate into ions that act as superfine abrasives on the surface.",
