    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, thanks. Could you expand a bit more on the specific enzyme-mediated steps? I'm particularly interested in the transition from the sulfoxides to the sulfenic acids, and maybe how the suddenness of the reaction—that almost 'sneaky' onset of the stinging—is tied to the way the gas disperses. It's such an abrupt change from a normal smell to intense irritation.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The enzyme alliinase specifically cleaves the sulfur-methionine-like bond in alliin to generate sulfenic acids as intermediate products. These unstable intermediates are almost instantly converted by another enzyme, lipoxygenase, into the final propanethial S-oxide. The abruptness of your reaction stems from this rapid cascade and the propagation speed of the vapor, which can trigger irritation within seconds due to its high diffusivity.",
      "source": "target",
